WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.946 --> 00:00:14.247
My name is Mike Matros.

00:00:14.247 --> 00:00:15.815
I'm the owner, inventor, and founder

00:00:15.815 --> 00:00:18.051
of RedLine Gear Cleaning.

00:00:18.051 --> 00:00:21.488
We are a mobile, on-site
gear-cleaning company.

00:00:21.488 --> 00:00:22.789
Today, we're going to
be taking you through

00:00:22.789 --> 00:00:24.758
the entire process of what is involved

00:00:24.758 --> 00:00:26.993
in cleaning, inspecting,

00:00:26.993 --> 00:00:29.062
and putting firefighting
gear back in service.

00:00:29.062 --> 00:00:31.197
One thing I wasn't really aware of

00:00:31.197 --> 00:00:32.465
when I took the job as a firefighter

00:00:32.465 --> 00:00:35.301
was the cancer problem that's
plaguing the fire service.

00:00:35.301 --> 00:00:36.403
Over the last 20 years,

00:00:36.403 --> 00:00:39.506
66% of all firefighter line-of-duty deaths

00:00:39.506 --> 00:00:41.174
have been related to cancer.

00:00:41.174 --> 00:00:43.877
It's not if I get cancer,
it's when I get cancer.

00:00:43.877 --> 00:00:45.445
That particular risk and exposure

00:00:45.445 --> 00:00:47.547
is something that we really
want to try and mitigate.

00:00:47.547 --> 00:00:49.482
If the environment is toxic

00:00:49.482 --> 00:00:51.518
to the point where you
have to wear your air pack,

00:00:51.518 --> 00:00:52.385
that's an exposure.

00:00:52.385 --> 00:00:55.021
Because if your lungs
cannot handle the exposure,

00:00:55.021 --> 00:00:57.390
your gear is absorbing all
those exposures into it,

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:58.892
and that needs to be cleaned and washed.

00:00:58.892 --> 00:01:02.328
So, the mobile extraction unit
is actually a patented design

00:01:02.328 --> 00:01:05.965
that allows the truck to come
directly to the fire station

00:01:05.965 --> 00:01:07.834
to be a mobile laundry facility

00:01:07.834 --> 00:01:10.370
that specializes in
cleaning firefighting gear.

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:13.706
It contains our water, our electric,

00:01:13.706 --> 00:01:15.942
big commercial extractors, dryers,

00:01:15.942 --> 00:01:17.610
equipment to clean helmets and air packs,

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:19.012
all specialized equipment needed

00:01:19.012 --> 00:01:21.481
to clean firefighting gear specifically.

00:01:21.481 --> 00:01:23.716
First step for us is to
assess the firehouse,

00:01:23.716 --> 00:01:26.886
and what that means could
be going locker to locker,

00:01:26.886 --> 00:01:28.188
looking at the gear,

00:01:28.188 --> 00:01:31.124
determining how exposed the gear is,

00:01:31.124 --> 00:01:32.692
determining what's in the guy's pockets,

00:01:32.692 --> 00:01:34.794
determining what gear needs to be cleaned.

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:39.232
This helmet is what we like to call

00:01:39.232 --> 00:01:41.101
in the fire service salty.

00:01:41.101 --> 00:01:42.735
This helmet is gross.

00:01:42.735 --> 00:01:44.370
It has seen a lot of smoke.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.172
It has seen a lot of contaminants.

00:01:46.172 --> 00:01:48.842
A lot of older guys tend to
call their gear salty gear

00:01:48.842 --> 00:01:50.310
as a badge of honor,

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:51.444
and they look at this gear

00:01:51.444 --> 00:01:53.413
as something that shows experience

00:01:53.413 --> 00:01:55.748
and shows they went to a
very hot, very involved fire

00:01:55.748 --> 00:01:57.050
and they got really dirty.

00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:58.485
That is something that, at RedLine,

00:01:58.485 --> 00:02:00.420
we're really trying to
change that culture.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:01.654
The badge of honor they're wearing

00:02:01.654 --> 00:02:03.356
is causing cancer to these guys.

00:02:03.356 --> 00:02:06.226
We're trying to tell them
that clean is the new salty.

00:02:06.226 --> 00:02:07.861
This is our ultrasonic machine.

00:02:07.861 --> 00:02:10.163
So, as you can see, we're
loading the helmets in,

00:02:10.163 --> 00:02:12.332
and this is what has
often been referred to

00:02:12.332 --> 00:02:14.067
as our deep-fryer for helmets.

00:02:15.435 --> 00:02:16.536
We use a degreaser.

00:02:16.536 --> 00:02:18.905
We also use a sanitizer in here

00:02:18.905 --> 00:02:21.508
that's going to clean, disinfect,
and sanitize your helmet

00:02:21.508 --> 00:02:23.176
to the point where it comes out,

00:02:23.176 --> 00:02:24.377
we just have to wipe it down.

00:02:26.112 --> 00:02:27.814
The helmet is probably
the most important part

00:02:27.814 --> 00:02:30.750
to a firefighter in terms of his gear.

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:32.986
RedLine's all firefighter-owned,
firefighter-operated.

00:02:32.986 --> 00:02:34.420
So some people like to say

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.322
we washed away all their experience.

00:02:36.322 --> 00:02:38.958
I like to think we added a
few years of life to them.

00:02:40.627 --> 00:02:43.062
We take a lot of time
washing and cleaning helmets.

00:02:43.062 --> 00:02:44.831
Now, that's probably the
most controversial thing

00:02:44.831 --> 00:02:46.299
we do as a company,

00:02:46.299 --> 00:02:48.268
because some people believe that helmet

00:02:48.268 --> 00:02:49.669
should never be touched.

00:02:52.772 --> 00:02:55.041
This gear is particularly very dirty.

00:02:55.041 --> 00:02:56.609
Really needs a good deep cleaning

00:02:56.609 --> 00:02:59.312
from both the helmet, hood,
jacket, pants, liners.

00:02:59.312 --> 00:03:01.915
All the gear really needs a
good, heavy deep cleaning,

00:03:01.915 --> 00:03:05.084
because this gear, it has
seen a lot of exposure.

00:03:06.486 --> 00:03:08.688
Once we've determined what
gear needs to be cleaned,

00:03:08.688 --> 00:03:10.256
that gear has to be inspected.

00:03:10.256 --> 00:03:12.625
It has to be separated
between the outer shells

00:03:12.625 --> 00:03:15.395
and the inner liners,
completely separated,

00:03:15.395 --> 00:03:17.230
keeping those wash cycles
completely different

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:20.133
so we don't have cross-contamination
between the cleaning.

00:03:20.133 --> 00:03:21.968
But while we're doing this,
we're inspecting the gear.

00:03:21.968 --> 00:03:23.169
We're looking for any rips,

00:03:23.169 --> 00:03:24.404
we're looking for burn marks,

00:03:24.404 --> 00:03:25.738
we're looking for holes in the gear.

00:03:25.738 --> 00:03:28.675
We're looking for anything that
can get a firefighter hurt.

00:03:29.642 --> 00:03:31.377
Here we have taken the inner liner,

00:03:31.377 --> 00:03:33.546
put it on our hydrostatic testing machine.

00:03:33.546 --> 00:03:36.216
This is a process that would
test a moisture barrier

00:03:36.216 --> 00:03:38.985
of a firefighter suit to make
sure that there's no leaks.

00:03:38.985 --> 00:03:41.988
Or any problems with the moisture barrier,

00:03:41.988 --> 00:03:43.456
to make sure that water isn't going to get

00:03:43.489 --> 00:03:45.825
to a firefighter's skin
and cause steam burns.

00:03:47.894 --> 00:03:50.863
The next step would be
to hand-clean the gear.

00:03:51.331 --> 00:03:53.466
We use a product called Decon7

00:03:53.466 --> 00:03:56.135
that we scrub gear to
the point where we get

00:03:56.135 --> 00:03:58.771
most of the contaminants
off the gear initially.

00:04:04.811 --> 00:04:07.080
This is an important part
for us as part of the process

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:08.881
to get rid of any bulk contaminants

00:04:08.881 --> 00:04:11.384
that would be on that gear,
whether it be insulation

00:04:11.384 --> 00:04:13.853
or anything else they might
run into in the field.

00:04:16.289 --> 00:04:18.157
A firefighter's gear needs to be cleaned

00:04:18.157 --> 00:04:19.459
after every exposure.

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:21.828
As you can see, it gets pretty nasty.

00:04:26.299 --> 00:04:27.300
After the inspection,

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:29.702
the inner liners are then separated out.

00:04:29.702 --> 00:04:31.871
They're turned inside out,
so this way we're washing

00:04:31.871 --> 00:04:34.407
the part that is against your skin first.

00:04:34.407 --> 00:04:36.676
Those would then go into
our commercial extractor

00:04:36.676 --> 00:04:38.978
and be washed through
our five-cycle program.

00:04:38.978 --> 00:04:41.281
We use cold water for washing gear.

00:04:41.281 --> 00:04:43.983
This not only helps the cleaning process,

00:04:43.983 --> 00:04:45.985
but at the same time it keeps the colors

00:04:45.985 --> 00:04:47.920
and stuff in the fabric true.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:50.556
We can fit six sets of firefighting gear

00:04:50.556 --> 00:04:52.525
into these machines
because they're so large.

00:04:52.625 --> 00:04:56.095
That's 12 pieces of gear
going into each extractor.

00:04:56.262 --> 00:05:00.533
After both the inner
shells and the outer shells

00:05:00.533 --> 00:05:01.868
are cleaned through our extractor,

00:05:01.868 --> 00:05:03.903
they then go into their respected dryer.

00:05:03.903 --> 00:05:05.538
We're able to dry both the liners

00:05:05.538 --> 00:05:07.573
and outer shells in one hour.

00:05:12.979 --> 00:05:16.149
We lay out the boots that
have to get hand-scrubbed,

00:05:16.149 --> 00:05:18.017
and we are also fogging them,

00:05:18.017 --> 00:05:19.452
spraying them with a disinfectant.

00:05:19.452 --> 00:05:20.953
It's going to neutralize,
it's going to kill,

00:05:20.953 --> 00:05:22.221
it's going to disinfect anything

00:05:22.221 --> 00:05:24.390
those boots might have
come in contact with.

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:26.459
One of the key elements
to a firefighter's gear

00:05:26.459 --> 00:05:28.127
is the hood they wear
underneath their helmet,

00:05:28.127 --> 00:05:29.762
and it goes through.
That protects their ears,

00:05:29.762 --> 00:05:31.264
that protects their helmet.

00:05:31.264 --> 00:05:33.333
People forget to even clean these.

00:05:33.333 --> 00:05:35.735
Anything that's going to be
exposed and touched to your skin

00:05:35.735 --> 00:05:37.804
should be washed the
same way as if you would

00:05:37.804 --> 00:05:40.340
the liners that are next to your skin.

00:05:42.775 --> 00:05:44.377
The water that comes off this extractor

00:05:44.377 --> 00:05:46.179
is like a sludge, like a mud,

00:05:46.179 --> 00:05:47.947
so anybody who believes the cancer problem

00:05:47.947 --> 00:05:49.215
in the fire service isn't real,

00:05:49.215 --> 00:05:50.783
I encourage you to see the wastewater

00:05:50.783 --> 00:05:52.385
that comes off this gear.

00:05:52.385 --> 00:05:55.588
It is nasty, it is black, it is like mud.

00:05:55.588 --> 00:05:57.023
So at the end of every job,

00:05:57.023 --> 00:06:00.793
it's important for us to
show what the wastewater

00:06:00.793 --> 00:06:02.228
and what the situation is like

00:06:02.228 --> 00:06:04.464
in terms of what's coming off the gear.

00:06:04.464 --> 00:06:06.099
This is the stuff that's
coming off your gear.

00:06:06.099 --> 00:06:08.301
This is stuff we just cleaned, just now,

00:06:08.301 --> 00:06:11.571
that has loaded with soot,
loaded with chemicals,

00:06:11.571 --> 00:06:13.906
loaded with stuff that's
causing cancer to these guys.

00:06:13.906 --> 00:06:16.342
And we make it a point
to show these filters

00:06:16.342 --> 00:06:18.644
to all of our customers,
to all the firefighters,

00:06:18.644 --> 00:06:21.581
to know just how dirty their gear was

00:06:21.581 --> 00:06:23.549
and how important cleaning their gear is.

00:06:24.684 --> 00:06:26.986
Our filtration process is so in depth

00:06:26.986 --> 00:06:30.156
that it allows us to take
really dirty water, filter it,

00:06:30.156 --> 00:06:31.657
run it through a number of filters,

00:06:31.657 --> 00:06:32.825
run it through a full process

00:06:32.825 --> 00:06:35.928
that makes that water
completely clean, reusable,

00:06:35.928 --> 00:06:38.664
prevents us from putting toxic products

00:06:38.664 --> 00:06:39.966
back into the environment,

00:06:39.966 --> 00:06:41.801
and makes it a sustainable solution

00:06:41.801 --> 00:06:44.270
that we can keep using and
be very environmental-safe

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:46.305
after we clean very toxic gear.

00:06:49.175 --> 00:06:51.944
Today, the fire service is
made up of a lot of chemicals.

00:06:51.944 --> 00:06:53.646
In the form of combustion at a fire,

00:06:53.646 --> 00:06:56.682
every single room now has
plastics, has particleboard,

00:06:56.682 --> 00:06:58.384
has different materials that are burning.

00:06:58.384 --> 00:07:00.453
It's causing the fires to burn hotter,

00:07:00.453 --> 00:07:01.988
to burn more violently,

00:07:01.988 --> 00:07:04.590
and the smoke is thick,
nasty, black smoke.

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:08.027
So while the fire service
has been around for years,

00:07:08.027 --> 00:07:10.329
the makeup and the exposures
of the fire service

00:07:10.329 --> 00:07:12.265
are very different nowadays.

00:07:16.836 --> 00:07:18.137
Our final product is taking

00:07:18.137 --> 00:07:20.339
all 10 pieces of gear that was cleaned,

00:07:20.339 --> 00:07:21.441
putting it back together,

00:07:21.441 --> 00:07:23.876
putting it in a gear bag, ready to go.

00:07:23.876 --> 00:07:26.012
Boots are on bottom, pants, jacket,

00:07:26.012 --> 00:07:27.513
hoods and gloves tucked in the side,

00:07:27.513 --> 00:07:28.514
helmet on the top,

00:07:28.514 --> 00:07:31.050
as one complete package, ready to go.

00:07:33.386 --> 00:07:35.188
We can't control
the dangers of the job,

00:07:35.354 --> 00:07:37.523
but we can control the exposure rate

00:07:37.523 --> 00:07:38.825
of what these guys are exposed to

00:07:38.825 --> 00:07:40.526
from a cancer-causing substance

00:07:40.526 --> 00:07:43.029
that's really killing this fire service.

00:07:43.029 --> 00:07:44.964
I don't need to see soot and dust

00:07:44.964 --> 00:07:46.966
all over a guy's helmet and gear

00:07:46.966 --> 00:07:48.868
to know he knows how to do his job.

00:07:48.868 --> 00:07:51.771
I want to see a guy who's
clean, got his uniform in shape,

00:07:51.771 --> 00:07:53.806
everything is properly ready to go,

00:07:53.806 --> 00:07:56.742
he's able to perform his job
to the best of his ability.

00:07:56.742 --> 00:07:59.011
That's a salty firefighter.

